{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not curse the deaf.</b> I have only the deaf — whence do I include every person? Scripture says, ״בְּעַמְּךָ לֹא תָאֹר״ (Exodus 22). If so, why is 'deaf' stated? Just as the deaf is distinguished in that he is alive, so too everyone who is alive — excluding the dead, who is not alive (Sifra)." "<b>And before the blind you shall not place a stumbling block.</b> Before one who is blind in a matter, do not give advice that is not fitting for him. Do not say, 'Sell your field and buy yourself a donkey' — while you circle around him and take it from him (ibid.)." "<b>And you shall fear your Elohim.</b> Because this matter is not given over to creatures to know whether this one's intention is for good or for harm, and he could evade and say, 'I intended it for good,' therefore it is stated about it, ״וְיָרֵאתָ מֵאֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — who knows your thoughts. And so with any matter given over to the heart of the person doing it, where the rest of the creatures cannot know it, ״וְיָרֵאתָ מֵאֱלֹהֶיךָ״ is stated about it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:09.671064584Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}